# Places where this engine deliberately disagrees with SQLite.
#
# Every record here is `onlyif qe`, so `tools/gen_expected.py` never touches it
# and `tools/fuzz_queries.py` never generates anything in these shapes. Each one
# is a design decision, and each is written down here so that a future
# disagreement in the differential corpus can be recognised as a bug rather
# than shrugged off as "SQLite is just like that".
#
# The recurring theme: SQLite is dynamically typed and has no BOOLEAN, DATE or
# DECIMAL type. This engine is statically typed, so it follows the standard
# where the two part ways.

load people data/people.csv

# 1. CASE unifies its branch types; SQLite returns whichever branch fired.
#
#    Standard SQL gives a CASE expression one result type, the unification of
#    every branch. With a REAL branch and an INTEGER branch that type is REAL,
#    so the division below is floating-point and yields 0.5. SQLite hands back
#    the INTEGER 1 from the ELSE and does integer division, yielding 0.
onlyif qe
query R nosort
SELECT (CASE WHEN 1 = 2 THEN 1.5 ELSE 1 END) / 2
----
0.500

# 2. Floats render in shortest round-trip form, not to 15 significant digits.
#
#    SQLite prints 0.3 here, which does not round-trip back to the value it
#    holds. PostgreSQL 12+ and DuckDB both switched to shortest round-trip for
#    the same reason: the printed form should reproduce the stored value.
onlyif qe
query T nosort
SELECT CAST(0.1 + 0.2 AS TEXT)
----
0.30000000000000004

# 3. A literal in an IN list is folded into the column's type.
#
#    SQLite compares across storage classes and finds no match, so `2 IN (1,'2')`
#    is false there. Here the string literal is folded to INT32 at bind time, so
#    it matches. This is the same literal folding that makes `WHERE d >
#    '2024-01-01'` compare dates, and it only ever applies to literals -- two
#    columns of mismatched type remain an error.
onlyif qe
query I nosort
SELECT 2 IN (1, '2')
----
1

# 4. Integer arithmetic is evaluated at 64 bits regardless of column width.
#
#    `id` and `salary` are both INT32 because the CSV loader saw values that fit
#    in 32 bits -- a storage decision, not a request for 32-bit arithmetic.
#    Multiplying them overflows an i32 but is computed exactly at i64.
onlyif qe
query I nosort
SELECT id * salary * (salary - 36) FROM people WHERE id = 7
----
141112216000

# 5. Comparisons do NOT widen, so an INT32 column stays INT32 on the left of a
#    predicate. That keeps zone-map bounds and (later) dictionary codes
#    comparable in their native width.
onlyif qe
query I rowsort
SELECT id FROM people WHERE salary > 190000
----
2
5
